For thousands of years, humans built their world from what the earth gave them.

Clothing was made from wool, hemp, cotton, linen, leather.
Homes were built from stone, hardwood, clay, metal.
Tools, furniture, and everyday objects were designed to last, age well, and be repaired.

These materials weren’t perfect, but they were real.

Then, in the last century, everything changed.

Natural fibres were replaced with synthetics.
Hardwoods were replaced with particleboard and MDF.
Leather was replaced with plastic coated as “vegan”.
PVC replaced wood.
Polyester replaced wool.

Not because they were better for people
but because they were cheaper, faster, and easier to scale.

We gained convenience.
We lost craft, durability, breathability, and honesty.

A wool garment regulates temperature.
A plastic one traps heat.

A hardwood table can last generations.
Particleboard crumbles when it gets wet.

Leather ages and tells a story.
Synthetic leather cracks and peels.

Somewhere along the way, we accepted imitation as progress.

This isn’t about nostalgia.
It’s about remembering what works.

Natural materials existed long before marketing departments, trend cycles, and planned obsolescence, and they survived because they were functional, resilient, and aligned with the human body.

The future doesn’t need more plastic pretending to be natural.
It needs better design, better materials, and more intention.
